  "proposal": "filename: docs/savings-basics.md\n---\nA savings account is a simple way to store money safely.  \nWhen you deposit money into your savings account, it goes into a balance that grows over time.  \nDeposits can be made in cash or through a bank transfer.  \nThe bank pays you interest on your balance, which means you earn money just for keeping your funds in the account.  \nInterest is usually small but adds up over time, helping your savings grow.  \nYou can check your balance at any time through your bank app or online portal.  \nBalances are updated automatically after every deposit or withdrawal.  \nWithdrawing money from your savings account means taking funds out of your balance.  \nYou can withdraw money in cash or by writing a check.  \nWithdrawals may have limits or require a fee, depending on the bank.  \nInterest rates vary by bank and account type, so it's good to compare options.  \nBalancing your savings helps you plan for future expenses or goals.  \nYou can set up automatic deposits to build your balance over time.  \nInterest is typically calculated monthly and added to your balance.  \nIf you withdraw money, your balance decreases and interest on the remaining amount continues.  \nThe balance is always the total of all deposits minus all withdrawals.  \nYou can monitor your balance to avoid overdrawing or missing interest.  \nUnderstanding deposits, withdrawals, interest, and balances helps you manage your money wisely.  \nSavings accounts are a great first step toward building financial stability.",
